    body: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A useful small-town Texas trip starts by dropping the idea that every courthouse square is the same. Settlement patterns, railroads, ranching, cotton, oil, immigration and highway construction left different fingerprints across the state. A Hill Country town with German roots, an East Texas timber town, a Panhandle railroad center and a South Texas ranch community may share a grid and a courthouse while telling completely different stories." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The Texas Historical Commission's Main Street program is a good framework for understanding why historic downtowns matter. The program focuses on preserving and revitalizing commercial districts because these blocks hold architecture, civic institutions and local businesses in one walkable place. For a visitor, that means the square is not just a photo stop; it is often the fastest way to understand how the town developed and what it is trying to preserve." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Start with the courthouse square, then slow down" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Arrive early enough to walk before the busiest part of the day. Look beyond the courthouse itself: old banks, theaters, hardware stores, hotels, railroad depots and fraternal buildings often explain the local economy better than a generic visitor brochure. Read historical markers, but also compare what the buildings are used for now. A district that still functions for residents feels different from one preserved only as scenery." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "County and local museums are especially valuable when they are small enough to be specific. Ask about the industries that built the town, the disasters or booms residents still remember, and the communities whose stories are easy to miss from the courthouse lawn. A thirty-minute museum stop can make the next two hours of walking far more legible." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Eat something that belongs to the place" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The best food stop does not have to be famous. A bakery tied to Czech or German settlement, a barbecue joint with regional sausage, a South Texas breakfast counter, a pie café or a long-running lunch special can connect the trip to local history. Ask what people order regularly rather than chasing a dish designed for tourists. The point is not novelty; it is learning what the town has chosen to keep." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Historic dance halls work the same way. They are more revealing when they are active venues, not empty landmarks. Check the calendar, buy a ticket when appropriate and treat the hall as a living community space. Gruene is an obvious example, but the larger lesson applies across Central Texas: music architecture makes more sense when people are actually dancing on the floor." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Use local events as anchors, not excuses to overpack" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "County fairs, rodeos, heritage festivals, courthouse lightings and high-school football games can turn a normal weekend into the best time to understand a town. They can also make parking, lodging and restaurant capacity tight. Pick one anchor event and leave room around it rather than stacking every attraction into a schedule that requires constant driving." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A Friday-night football game deserves special context. In many communities the stadium is a civic gathering point, but visitors should treat it as a real school event rather than a themed attraction. Buy a ticket, follow local rules, respect student participants and use the evening to observe how the community gathers rather than turning people into scenery." },
      { type: "heading", text: "The road between towns is part of the experience" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Farm-to-market roads, river crossings, cotton fields, limestone ridges, grain elevators and roadside churches often explain why neighboring towns developed differently. A loop of two or three communities gives those transitions time to register. Six towns in a day usually produces six parking lots and very little understanding." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Pair the town with one nearby landscape: a state park, river, lake, scenic overlook or historic site. That prevents the square from carrying the entire trip and connects the built environment to the geography that shaped it. In the Hill Country, for example, a town visit makes more sense when limestone, springs and ranch roads are part of the same day." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Stay overnight when evening changes the town" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Some places reveal their best character after day-trippers leave. A local inn, an evening concert, a quiet courthouse square and breakfast the next morning can turn a checklist stop into a real visit. Overnight stays also make it easier to support businesses that preserve historic districts without forcing every meal and activity into peak midday hours." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The strongest small-town itinerary is therefore simple: choose a town for a specific reason, learn its history, eat something connected to the place, attend one community activity if the timing works, pair it with a nearby landscape and leave enough unscheduled time to notice what makes it different. That approach produces fewer stops and a much better picture of Texas." },
    ],
