    readingMinutes: 11,
    tags: ["texas wildlife", "wildlife encounters", "coyote safety", "alligator safety"],
    body: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The safest Texas wildlife encounter is usually the least dramatic one. Texas Parks and Wildlife Department guidance repeatedly comes back to distance, removing attractants and avoiding feeding or handling wild animals. Most animals encountered around parks, roads and neighborhoods are trying to move through habitat, find food or avoid people. Problems grow when humans close the distance, leave reliable food sources or allow pets to initiate contact." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The exact response depends on the species, but a useful first sequence is consistent: stop approaching, bring children and pets under control, leave the animal an escape route and remove food or trash that may encourage it to return. If an animal is injured, trapped, behaving abnormally or repeatedly creating a conflict in a developed area, use the appropriate wildlife, animal-control or public-health resource instead of treating the encounter as a do-it-yourself rescue." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Deer, hogs and coyotes require different kinds of space" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "White-tailed deer are a major road hazard, especially around dawn and dusk. When one crosses, slow down and look for others rather than accelerating as soon as the first animal clears the lane. In neighborhoods, feeding deer can concentrate animals, alter behavior and create additional conflicts. Observation at a distance is safer than turning a yard into a predictable feeding site." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Feral hogs deserve room, particularly when piglets are present or dogs are involved. Do not approach for photographs or try to drive hogs into a corner. Coyotes require a different neighborhood strategy: TPWD recommends eliminating food attractants, securing trash and pet food, supervising small pets and using appropriate hazing when a coyote has become too comfortable around people. Feeding is the behavior most likely to undermine those boundaries." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Alligators are a shoreline-management problem, not a photo opportunity" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "TPWD alligator-safety guidance tells visitors to keep substantial distance, never feed or harass alligators and closely supervise children near water. Pets should be kept away from the water's edge in alligator habitat because their size and movement can attract attention. An alligator that is simply visible is not automatically a nuisance animal; behavior and context matter." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "At parks and managed sites, follow posted restrictions and staff instructions rather than assuming a familiar-looking shoreline is safe everywhere. Do not throw food into the water, attempt to provoke movement for a picture or stand between an animal and the water. If an alligator is blocking a developed area or behaving in a way that concerns visitors, report it through the site's established process." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Smaller mammals create most problems through food and shelter" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Raccoons, opossums, skunks, foxes and armadillos often become neighborhood topics because they find pet food, unsecured trash, crawl spaces, decks or irrigated lawns. The long-term solution is usually exclusion and attractant control rather than repeated feeding or close handling. Seal structural access only after confirming animals are not trapped inside, and use professional help for situations involving occupied structures, young animals or potential disease exposure." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Skunks signal discomfort before spraying; if one stamps, raises its tail or focuses on you, stop approaching and give it room. Armadillos can damage lawns through digging but are still wild animals, not hand-removal projects. Bobcats and foxes are typically more interested in avoiding people, yet small pets should be supervised where predators are active." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Bats deserve special handling caution" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Never handle a grounded or indoor bat with bare hands. If a bat may have contacted a person or pet, especially where the contact is uncertain, use current public-health and veterinary guidance rather than releasing or discarding the animal without advice. The concern is not that every bat is dangerous; it is that potential exposure decisions are time-sensitive and should be made with professionals." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The same principle applies to wildlife that appears sick, unusually aggressive or unable to move normally. Do not diagnose it from a few feet away. Increase distance, keep people and pets out of the area and contact the relevant local or state resource." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Design the property so wildlife has fewer reasons to stay" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Secure trash, feed pets indoors when practical, remove leftover food, protect gardens appropriately and close access to attics or crawl spaces. Those actions reduce repeated conflicts more effectively than chasing individual animals away after they have learned a property provides food or shelter." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Texas wildlife viewing is better when distance is treated as part of the experience. Binoculars, zoom lenses and patient observation produce better behavior and safer photographs than approaching. The goal is not to make wild animals disappear from Texas neighborhoods and parks; it is to keep encounters from becoming habituation, injury or emergency response." },
    ],
