    id: "gateway-pests",
    brandId: "texasdefined",
    slug: "texas-pests-homeowners-should-know",
    title: "Texas Pests Homeowners Should Know: Prevention, Identification and When to Call a Pro",
    dek: "A Texas A&M AgriLife-informed homeowner guide to fire ants, mosquitoes, termites, roaches, scorpions, rodents and other recurring pest problems.",
    category: "home-garden",
    hero: images.town,
    authorId: "a-hollis",
    publishedAt: "2026-08-20",
    readingMinutes: 11,
    tags: ["texas pests", "home pests", "termites texas", "fire ants texas", "integrated pest management"],
    body: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Texas pest pressure changes with region, rainfall, temperature, landscaping and the way a house is built. A Gulf Coast home may fight moisture-loving insects differently from a dry West Texas property, while new construction and older pier-and-beam houses create different entry points. The useful goal is not to eliminate every insect outdoors. It is to prevent pests from becoming a health, structural or persistent indoor problem." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Texas A&M AgriLife Extension's integrated pest management approach starts with identification, prevention and the least disruptive effective control. That matters because a treatment chosen for the wrong pest can waste money, expose people or pets unnecessarily and leave the actual source untouched. Before spraying, determine what is present, where it is coming from and whether moisture, food, shelter or structural access is sustaining it." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Moisture and food explain many indoor invasions" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Large outdoor cockroaches, silverfish and other moisture-associated insects often point toward damp harborages, leaks, drains, mulch or exterior gaps. German cockroaches are a different problem because they can establish indoor populations and require more targeted management. Treating every roach sighting as the same infestation misses the biology that determines whether sanitation, exclusion, baiting or professional treatment is most appropriate." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Pantry pests offer a simple version of the same lesson. The adult insects seen in a cabinet are not the source; an infested grain, flour, pet-food or stored-product package usually is. Removing and cleaning around the source does more than repeatedly killing visible adults. Fleas likewise require attention to pets, resting areas and the larger life cycle rather than only the room where a person noticed bites." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Termites and rodents justify faster escalation" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Subterranean termites are important because damage can remain hidden while colonies work behind finishes or around foundations. Mud tubes, damaged wood or swarming insects deserve proper identification. A homeowner can reduce conducive conditions such as moisture and wood-to-soil contact, but suspected structural infestation is a strong reason to use a qualified pest professional instead of relying on a general-purpose spray." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Rodents combine property damage with sanitation concerns. Roof rats and other rodents can exploit small openings around rooflines, vents, utility penetrations and garages. Effective control means finding entry points, reducing food and water, and removing animals without sealing them into the structure. Chewed wiring, contaminated insulation or repeated attic activity can move the problem beyond routine homeowner maintenance." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Outdoor pests are best managed by reducing favorable conditions" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Mosquito control begins with water. Gutters, plant saucers, toys, buckets and other containers can hold enough water to support breeding. Emptying or managing standing water reduces pressure without blanketing the landscape with pesticide. Fire-ant management also benefits from identification and a strategy matched to the site; indiscriminate disturbance can split colonies or move activity rather than solve the problem." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Ticks and chiggers are more likely around tall vegetation, brush, wildlife movement and certain outdoor activities. Mow and manage high-use areas, create separation between play spaces and dense brush where practical, and check people and pets after exposure. Wasps and hornets deserve added caution when nests are near doors, eaves, play areas or routine maintenance routes, particularly for anyone with a history of severe allergic reaction." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Scorpions, spiders and occasional invaders need context" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Scorpions are more common in some Texas regions and can follow insect prey into garages and homes. Exclusion, clutter reduction and management of the insects they feed on can matter as much as treating individual scorpions. Most spiders are predators of other arthropods, so identification is more useful than assuming every web requires chemical control." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Carpenter ants, wood-boring beetles, grubs, armyworms, aphids and scale insects all create different problems and require different thresholds for action. A plant or lawn can tolerate some insect activity without treatment. Integrated pest management asks whether the pest is actually causing unacceptable damage before choosing a control." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Know when the problem is bigger than routine prevention" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Call a qualified professional when there is suspected structural damage, a persistent indoor infestation, a stinging-insect nest in a high-risk location, rodents in inaccessible building cavities or any situation where treatment would require products or access you cannot use safely. Public-health concerns, severe allergies and uncertain identification are also good reasons to escalate instead of experimenting." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "For most homes, the durable routine is less dramatic: fix leaks, manage standing water, store food securely, maintain screens and door sweeps, seal appropriate exterior gaps, keep vegetation from creating hidden moisture against the structure and inspect changes before they become large infestations. Prevention is usually cheaper and more targeted than reacting after a pest population is established." },
    ],
